Speaker diarization (engine/diar, the ox-diar engine) runs FluidAudio (https://github.com/FluidInference/FluidAudio,
Apache-2.0, Copyright Fluid Inference; its fastcluster and VBx notices ship in engine/licenses/) on the Core ML models
of FluidInference/speaker-diarization-coreml, which scripts/fetch-models.sh downloads at a pinned revision. Those
models are modified Core ML conversions, made by Fluid Inference, of the pyannote/speaker-diarization-community-1
pipeline (https://huggingface.co/pyannote/speaker-diarization-community-1, CC-BY-4.0): pyannote's segmentation model,
WeSpeaker's ResNet34-LM speaker embedding model (Wang et al., "Wespeaker: A research and production oriented speaker
embedding learning toolkit", ICASSP 2023; trained on VoxCeleb, CC-BY-4.0 upstream), and the PLDA parameters of BUT
Speech@FIT (Brno University of Technology, CC-BY-4.0, commercial use included). Credit: pyannote, WeSpeaker, BUT
Speech@FIT and Fluid Inference. The model repository's own LICENSE, NOTICE.md, README.md (which carries the citations the
licence asks us to retain) and PROVENANCE.md are downloaded beside the models by scripts/fetch-models.sh. Licence: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).
